## The August move

For roughly two weeks in August 2026, self-propelled transporters walked six prefabricated concourse modules across a closed DFW taxiway to their foundations at what will become Terminal F. The largest was 278 feet by 136 feet, weighed 3,320 tons, and was surveyed into position to a three-quarter-inch tolerance.[^1] The photographs traveled well: enormous white forms on rubber tires, a lit taxiway, the airfield asleep. It is a beautiful piece of construction, and it hides the argument of this report.

Every consequential decision about that first module was finalized months earlier, in a factory, at a design-lock date the public record does not name. The metaphor in this report's own title names a deadline that had already passed, for part of the building, on the day the modules were set. The operative deadline at Terminal F is release-for-fabrication, per module — and DFW's board is making design decisions against a construction method whose language of flexibility is inverted.

That inversion changes the recommendation set. Post-release modular is not more flexible than stick-built; it is more change-hostile. The tenant's siting decisions have already said what Terminal F is expected to carry in its first years — Provisions by Admirals Club in F, the largest Admirals Club ever built in Terminal C, Flagship Check-In in Terminal D.[^2] And when the record of purpose-built hub terminals is read completely, hardest analogue included, the design instruction that survives is a hybrid. Generic hub bones now. Priced, conditioned premium shells at a few positions, backed by a signed cost-recovery instrument. And a written register, module by module, of what is still alive to decide — with a named owner, a real contract instrument, and a confirmed procurement path.

## Executive summary

1. **The deadline in the title is misleading.** The reversibility clock at Terminal F is set by release-for-fabrication, per module, weeks to months before any concrete cures. For the six modules placed in August 2026,[^1] it has already passed. Every remaining recommendation in this report is timed to the next module's fabrication release, not to a generic pour date. The register must be prospective — anchored to the first module still inside the fabrication window — not a change log for modules already committed.

2. **Post-release modular is more change-hostile than stick-built, not less.** Seven of the twelve independent research agents on this run — the Chief Engineer, Operations Analyst, Quantitative Analyst, Contrarian, Council Rams, Airport COO, and Aviation Historian — converged from different starting points on the same reading: once a module clears release-for-fabrication, downstream scope changes propagate across the fabrication line rather than being absorbed within a single trade. The 30% cost saving and 30% schedule saving DFW attributes to the method[^3] is a repetition dividend, contingent on identical modules and on the Innovation Next+ JV contract accommodating owner-directed template changes at repeat-part cost. No independent audit against a stick-built counterfactual exists in the public record, and every bespoke premium feature erodes the dividend. The precise change-cost multiplier is analyst judgment, not a measured figure, and this report does not state one.

3. **The tenant's announced posture is a strong signal, not a settled U&L instruction.** American has placed a 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club — its largest anywhere — in Terminal C near the Skylink station, a Flagship Check-In in Terminal D, and a scaled-down Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go in Terminal F.[^2] Twelve research agents corroborated the announcement, but all of them trace to a single American press release and secondary reporting, and the long-term U&L status of the Provisions designation is unconfirmed. The design brief should treat the announcement as the strongest available carrier signal about Terminal F's first years, not a permanent settlement — and should engage two alternative readings on the page: sequencing, and Terminal F as an Arrivals-premium node on a Skylink spine.

4. **American is expanding the connecting bank, not softening it — and the sizing debate about that is genuine.** The April 2026 restructure moved DFW from 9 daily banks to 13.[^4] The Airline Commercial Strategist and Operations Analyst read that as denser peaks through bank contiguity; the Airport COO reads it as a flatter, mid-day-loaded operation. Terminal F sizing hinges on which is closer to right. This report sizes to the denser-peak reading: the Q2 2026 operating record — DFW still second-worst among busy US airports on departure performance, block times lengthened across 145 markets[^5] — is not the signature of a flattened peak. The rebank's peak-flattening effect is claimed, not proven. Decision D4 requires the COO to publish an annual re-test that either corroborates or unwinds the denser-peak sizing.

5. **The historical record on purpose-built hub terminals is mixed, and the mixed cases matter.** Six times since deregulation, a US airport has built a purpose-designed hub terminal on a long single-carrier commitment. Four were dehubbed a decade to fifteen years after opening — Pittsburgh 1992, dehubbed 2004;[^6] Cincinnati, peaked 2005, unwound through 2013;[^7] St. Louis, peaked 2001, cut in half by 2003;[^8] Cleveland, newer concourse dark in 2014.[^9] Detroit McNamara (2002) aged well because it encoded a hub *form* rather than a Northwest form.[^25] JetBlue's Terminal 5 at JFK (2008) is the harder case: an $875M terminal that has required a $200M international-arrivals extension in 2014 and a ~$100M premium refresh in 2025–26 — post-opening retrofit spend approaching one-third of original construction cost, because premium and international optionality were not priced into the original scope.[^34] Atlanta's Maynard Jackson / Concourse F (2012, $1.4B) is the counter-counterexample: a purpose-built premium international floor plate at a mega-connecting hub that has functioned as designed.[^35] Read together, the record argues for hub-generic bones *plus* priced, conditioned premium shells — not for either alone.

6. **The pour-locked decisions with the largest downstream cost of being wrong are geometric, not aesthetic.** MARS-capable stands at a defined minority of positions,[^10] a Federal Inspection Services floor plate sized to CBP's roughly 100 passengers per hour per double primary booth with 50–75-foot queue depth,[^11] checkpoint rooms dimensioned for next-generation CT throughput rather than 2015-era bins-and-belts,[^12] Skylink-node redundancy against a 6:15 a.m. single-loop failure,[^13] baggage-handling trunk cross-section, and a lounge-capable shell of structure, mechanical, and curtain-wall provision. None is a Flagship-tier build-out. All carry order-of-magnitude cost premiums the register must publish per line — the Quantitative Analyst has not yet delivered them, and the register cannot go to the Finance and Audit Committee without them.

7. **The airport's affordability arbitrage is being spent during the ramp.** DFW's cost per enplanement was $13.59 in FY25 and is projected at $16.99 in FY26 in the 2025 A/B bond Official Statement, as aggregated by DWU Consulting [FC-CHECK: verify both figures against the bondholder OS primary document].[^14] Outstanding debt was projected to grow from $7.2B in 2024 to $12.4B by FY29 as of the August 2024 rating action — nine months before Terminal F expanded from $1.6B/15 gates to $4B/31 gates; the post-expansion figure has not been published and is higher.[^15] DFW remains well below JFK, LAX, EWR, and ORD on CPE.[^16] The Airline Commercial Strategist's professional judgment is that connecting-hub CPE crossing the low $20s begins to change carrier behavior; the ledger contains no public CPE projection for FY27–FY30. The register requires a Finance and Audit Committee-set projected-CPE ceiling that the aggregate register-driven option premium must live inside. The number belongs to the Committee; the discipline of the ceiling belongs to this report.

8. **The most valuable governance artifact this run can produce is a written, module-by-module reversibility register, executed inside real contract instruments.** Not a "flexibility strategy." A short, priced list of options bought before each module's fabrication release, each with a named VP-level owner, an order-of-magnitude cost, a named funding source, named federal dependencies, and — where the space serves American — a Terminal F Reversibility Side Letter to the 2025 U&L amendment carrying a schedule of pre-priced options. It survives board turnover; it turns option-preservation from rhetoric into a specific act of governance; and it enforces the discipline that generic-geometry design is not a shopping list. Six decisions, addressed to six chairs, put the register on a real footing, and three requested board actions close the loop.

The rest of this report defends those eight claims, sets them against the strongest counter-case a sophisticated reader can build, and closes on the decisions that fall to DFW's board and CEO's office during the fabrication-release window that runs through 2030.

