## The argument

### The deadline is not the pour

The May 2025 announcement moved Terminal F from a $1.6 billion, 15-gate facility to a $4 billion, 31-gate program and extended American's Use and Lease Agreement from 2033 to 2043.[^17] Phase 1 opens in 2027; the full program completes in 2030. The reversibility conversation runs on the title's language: before the concrete cures.

For a modular build, that language is wrong. The concrete does not cure once. Each module's structure, curtain wall, mechanical risers, primary electrical service, and interior partitions are frozen in a factory, weeks to months before the module is transported and set. Once cutting, welding, and MEP first-fit begin, the module's dimensional identity is committed; changing a column grid or riser location after that triggers resequencing across every module downstream, not the marginal price of the change. DFW should publish the per-module release-for-fabrication milestones so the board can see which decisions are still live and which have quietly closed. That request alone is worth the run.

Marketing conflates the roughly 30% cost and schedule savings DFW attributes to the method[^3] with an unspecified third benefit called flexibility. There is no third benefit. The savings are a repetition dividend: the factory runs the same module over and over, same MEP tree, same wall thicknesses. Every bespoke premium feature — a double-height lounge volume above a gate hold, a curtain-wall break for a Flagship arrival vestibule — either forces an off-standard module that does not clear the factory at repeat-part cost, or forces the base module to carry provisions most positions do not need.

The dividend is also contingent on the contract. The Innovation Next+ JV is delivering Terminal C's nine-gate pier, Terminal A's renewal, and Terminal F concurrently, sharing craft labor, crane windows, and yard capacity across all three. Every template change registered against Terminal F competes for the same fabrication line, and the register's cadence depends on whether the JV's contract permits owner-directed template modifications at repeat-part cost or triggers a re-price cycle. That is a procurement question — Decision D0 — and it must be answered before the register's first module cycle begins.

Seven of the twelve research agents converged on the change-hostility reading even where their spending conclusions diverged — the Operations Analyst uses the same finding to argue the *opposite* recommendation, that the collapsed flex premium should be spent. A fair reading. It does not change what follows: the register is not optional.

### What the tenant has said, and what it has not

In July 2026, American announced its "next-generation premium experience at DFW." Three anchors. A 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club — the airline's largest ever — in Terminal C near the Skylink station. A Flagship Check-In near the D30 checkpoint in Terminal D, opening later in 2026, with separate entrance and dedicated premium processing. And in Terminal F, a Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go — explicitly a scaled-down concept, not a Flagship Lounge and not a full Admirals Club.[^2]

This is the single most-corroborated fact in the run — and every record traces to the same American press release. Convergence on one primary source, not twelve independent observations. What the announcement means for F's design brief is the run's sharpest disagreement. Three readings deserve the page.

**Reading one: revealed preference.** The tenant with the most information about its own 2043 network has spent its premium capital at C and D and told the airport, for now, what F is for: a bank-processing floor, the widebody feeder and pressure valve on the 13-bank restructure.

**Reading two: sequencing.** Terminal F does not open until 2027; C and D are the only places American *could* site premium capital in the near term. The Provisions designation is equally consistent with sequencing as with tiering. A 2032 announcement could add a Flagship-tier build at F.

**Reading three: Arrivals-premium.** The Virtual Chris brief argues that Skylink is becoming American's premium spine at DFW, and that F's premium role may be Arrivals rather than Departures — the transatlantic 787-9P passenger deplanes into a Flagship Arrivals suite in F, then Skylinks to C to connect or D to continue. Under that reading, the Terminal C siting says nothing about F's premium ceiling; it describes the shape of the network.

This report treats reading one as the strongest available *near-term* signal, and reading three as the option the register should quietly preserve at low marginal cost. Reading two is the reason none of the register's premium items should be conditioned on American's July 2026 posture holding through 2043. The register is the instrument that lets American change its mind — at American's expense — across all three readings.

American's own record supports the discipline. In March 2020 the airline closed every Flagship Lounge except JFK — LAX, DFW, MIA, ORD — and did not begin reopening the network until fall 2021.[^18] A design bet that has to survive to 2043 needs to survive several plausible repetitions of that cycle. Delta — whose Q4 2025 premium ticket revenue crossed main cabin for the first time in company history, on FY2025 premium revenue of $22.1 billion[^19] — has targeted 2028 for the ATL Delta One Lounge; industry observers attribute the timing to the difficulty of premium execution at a mega-connecting hub, though Delta has stated only the date [FC-CHECK: verify ATL Delta One Lounge program status as of August 2026].[^20]

None of this argues against premium as a fleet strategy. American's targets — narrowbody premium share from ~25% to ~40%, international lie-flat count up more than 50% by end of decade[^21] — are real and executable in the cabins, which the airport does not build. The airline is also shifting long-haul weight into DFW: 6% year-over-year capacity growth in Q3 2026 against a 13% reduction at London Heathrow, plus six new international routes for summer 2026.[^22] But premium's physical anchor at Terminal F, on American's public record, is a grab-and-go and a widebody-feeder concourse.

### Bank density, not bank flatness — a defended choice, not a physics claim

The other half of what American has told the airport is that Terminal F should be scoped against a denser connecting bank, not a flatter one — and this is where the design decision has to be *made*, not assumed. The December 2025 restructure moved DFW from 9 daily banks to 13, effective April 2026.[^4]

Inside this run, that fact carries two competing readings. The Airport COO reads the 13-bank messaging as a *flatter*, mid-day-loaded operation — banks spread across a longer window, peak concurrency reduced. The Airline Commercial Strategist and Operations Analyst read it the opposite way: bank contiguity amplifies gate concurrency at peaks. Terminal F sizing depends on which is correct.

This report adopts the denser-peak reading. American reported a 50% reduction in missed connections two weeks after launch — a self-reported figure from a two-week window. The Q2 2026 audit is more sober: American's DFW departure rate rose about 1% year over year, DFW remained second-worst among busy US airports on departure performance, and American lengthened scheduled block times by an average of six minutes across 145 markets network-wide, including CLT, DCA, and MIA — schedule surgery that improves reported on-time performance without changing the airborne product.[^5] That is not the operational signature of a flattened peak. It is the signature of a hub whose peak-day arithmetic is still demanding.

The read is not that the rebank failed. It is that its peak-flattening benefit is claimed, not proven. The building has to serve a bank operation carrying roughly 100,000 peak-day American customers on approximately 930 peak-day departures[^23] — with the connecting share contested between about 60% airport-level and roughly two-thirds carrier-level. The airport should cite both numbers, name the denominator each time, and size the pour-locked geometry against the higher share, because the connecting geometry is what fails on the bad morning. If the COO's flatter reading proves correct, D4's annual re-test catches the over-provision and unwinds it.

### The 6:15 a.m. sizing case

Here is the bad morning. Skylink runs on a two-minute headway with a nine-minute maximum interterminal transit during the day. Overnight, from 22:00 to 06:00, one loop shuts down for maintenance; transit on the single loop doubles to roughly fifteen minutes.[^13] Load onto that operating state an early-morning arrival bank from American's 13-bank day and the January 2023 winter storm that produced a DFW ground stop with 1,100+ cancellations on the peak day and 600+ the next.[^24] Put Terminal F's 31 gates and its Phase 1 Skylink station into that morning.

That is a *constructed* stress case, offered by the Airport COO — the precise timing is professional judgment, not a documented daily event. The register should adopt it as the formal sizing scenario for exactly that reason: a documented weekday morning is a system that already runs.

The scenario has a geometry test and an operating test. The Terminal F Skylink station's platform width, vertical circulation core count, and concourse-side queuing depth determine whether the building can absorb a 6:15 loop failure without backing passengers into the security checkpoint. The FIS primary hall is sized against CBP's roughly 100 passengers per hour per double booth and 50–75-foot queue standard[^11] — get the geometry wrong and the airport buys it back in CBP overtime and connect-time penalties for the life of the building. Checkpoint rooms must be dimensioned for next-generation CT lanes — 200 passengers per hour average, 330 at peak, against DHS's 300-per-lane target[^12] — because roller conveyor run length, secondary search depth, and ceiling height are pour-locked; casework and divest lanes are not. Gate concurrency across a bank is determined by whether stands are Code C, Code E, or MARS-capable; MARS lets one stand serve either a widebody or two narrowbody bank turns,[^10] and the enabling apron infrastructure is set at the pour.

The operating test is a tabletop, not a design memo. The 6:15 scenario has to be run against a staffing model, with the airport's airline and federal partners at the same table. If CBP will not staff the sized hall under the current Reimbursable Services Program status, the shell is a moot preservation. If TSA will not concur on the CT-lane geometry, the checkpoint room is a design intent without a permit. Both outcomes belong in Decision D4, not in a footnote.

The tabletop also settles one open disposition. Common Use Passenger Processing System (CUPPS)-capable gate hardware on a single-occupant terminal is a Contrarian-vs-COO disagreement: an option that pays back only if a future carrier ever occupies gates at F. This report sides with the COO: CUPPS stays on the register, because on a bad morning the alternative is manual reassignment at 6:15 a.m. across an exclusive-use envelope.

The 6:15 case is not a resilience bullet. It is the discipline: before each fabrication release, the design team demonstrates that the still-open decisions survive the morning the building is worst, with named federal partners signed onto the operating model around them.

### The historical spine, honestly counted

Purpose-built hub terminals on long single-carrier commitments are the design bet Terminal F is running. Since deregulation, that bet has been made roughly six times at American-scale hubs. The record is mixed, and the mixed cases are the ones that matter.

**Four ended badly.** Pittsburgh's 1992 X-shaped Midfield Terminal — approximately $1 billion, US Airways at 80%+ share, a twenty-five-year Use and Lease — was dehubbed in 2004 over a fee dispute the airport could not resolve; the stranded debt was carried until roughly 2019, and CPE nearly doubled by 2011.[^6] Cincinnati went from 22.7 million passengers and 600+ daily flights in 2005 to fewer than 6 million and under 180 daily by 2013.[^7] St. Louis fell from 500+ daily American/TWA flights in July 2001 to 207 by 2003, after American acquired TWA and the ORD slot restriction lifted.[^8] Cleveland's Concourse D — the newer hub concourse — went dark in May 2014; destinations dropped from 61 to 20.[^9]

**One aged well on hub-generic geometry.** Detroit McNamara, opened February 2002 for Northwest at roughly $1.2 billion, absorbed the 2008 Delta–Northwest merger without material retrofit.[^25] SmithGroup's linear midfield with satellite concourses was a hub *form*, not a Northwest form; any hub carrier's connecting bank could load it. The decision that made it survive cost nothing at design time: generic geometry inside carrier-specific finishes.

**And two cases that cut in opposite directions — one directly against the shell-preservation instinct.** JetBlue's Terminal 5 at JFK opened in 2008 at approximately $875 million as a purpose-built domestic terminal, and has since required a $200 million international arrivals concourse (T5i, 2014) and a roughly $100 million premium refresh in 2025–26 — post-opening retrofit spend approaching one-third of original construction cost, because premium and international optionality were not priced into the original scope.[^34] The aviation historian called T5 "the strongest available analogue to the Terminal F decision." Read straight, it argues *against* deferral: pricing that optionality into the 2027 scope is dramatically cheaper than pricing it into a 2033 retrofit. Atlanta's Maynard Jackson / Concourse F (2012, approximately $1.4 billion) is the counter-counter: a purpose-built premium international floor plate at a mega-connecting hub that has functioned as designed.[^35]

The recommendation this report lands on — hub-generic bones *plus* a small number of priced, conditioned premium shells at head-of-pier positions — is the one that survives T5, DTW, and PIT together: price a small set of premium-convertible shells at fabrication release, condition them on signed cost recovery, and refuse everything else.

The sample is six — directional evidence, not diagnostic. DFW is not Pittsburgh: American carried 82.6% of DFW's passengers in 2025 on top of a real Dallas–Fort Worth O&D economy of about 8 million people,[^26] the Use and Lease runs to 2043, and the §365 bankruptcy record shows American assumed 554 unexpired real-property leases in the 2011 filing against 12 rejected — legally cheap to walk from, commercially rare to actually do.[^27] The historical warning applies most sharply to the shape of the building, not the survival of the hub.

But it applies. Terminal F is being built at the moment American moved on hub structure and premium siting inside eight months. The design has to be legible to whatever version of American runs Terminal F in 2035, without a retrofit. DTW's discipline is available at essentially zero incremental cost; T5's warning at a small option premium — if the airport takes them now. The register is the difference between preserving the option and paying to build it.

### What generic geometry actually means, with per-item costs the register must publish

Generic geometry, honestly priced, is a short list. Each item below is an analyst-constructed order-of-magnitude range the Quantitative Analyst must confirm before the register goes to the Finance and Audit Committee. Not audit-grade; register-grade.

- **MARS-capable stands at a defined minority of widebody-reachable positions.** Fuel pit, PCA and 400 Hz risers, jetbridge foundation, apron lead-in geometry, all set at the apron pour under FAA AC 150/5300-13B.[^28] Analyst judgment: single-digit percent of per-stand base scope at pour; multiples if retrofitted. Federal dependency: FAA modification-of-standard letter where MARS geometry departs from the AC.
- **FIS floor plate and sterile-corridor width sized to CBP's Airport Technical Design Standard, not to a single carrier's 2027 international schedule.** American's redeployment of long-haul flying into DFW[^22] makes the FIS the single pour-locked decision with the largest 2043 international-capacity consequence. Analyst judgment: right-sizing costs low tens of millions inside a $4B program; retrofit an order of magnitude higher, on the ATL Concourse F record. Federal dependency: CBP Reimbursable Services Program agreement or staffing memo before the sized throughput is operable — a two-year negotiation, not a design directive.
- **Baggage-handling trunk cross-section sized against a peak connecting bank at 2035 volumes, not opening-year volumes.** Analyst judgment: oversizing at pour is a single-digit percent of BHS scope; retrofit is disruptive and materially higher.
- **Vertical MEP risers, grease-waste rough-ins, and structural allowance for a Flagship-tier lounge shell** — 25,000 to 40,000 square feet of preserved envelope with the plumbing and load path a future kitchen would need, deliberately not built out. Analyst judgment: order-of-magnitude cheaper at fabrication than retrofit into an operating concourse. Airline dependency: the Terminal F Reversibility Side Letter; without it, this is airport-side preload of tenant-improvement scope and the ratepayer carries it.
- **Skylink-node platform width, vertical circulation core count, and queuing depth sized for the 6:15 stress case.** Inside base Terminal F scope; the marginal cost is design-time, not construction-time.
- **Checkpoint rooms dimensioned for next-generation CT geometry.** Inside base scope; TSA concurrence on the room layout is on the critical path.
- **Dense sensor conduit, PoE++ ceiling drops, fiber diversity out of the terminal, camera mounts and data drops at every apron corner and jetbridge tunnel** — a common instrumentation baseline shipped through the module template. Analyst judgment: modest template premium if the JV contract accommodates repeat-part-cost changes (Decision D0); materially higher if not.
- **CUPPS-capable gate hardware** — resolved above, for the COO. Airline dependency: American IT participation in factory witness testing.

None of the above is a Flagship Lounge. None is a bet on American's 2033 premium build-out. All of it works for a tenant DFW has not met. The airport pays an option premium now — most of it at repeat-part cost inside the module template — and preserves the ability for American, or a future signatory, to fit out any of those envelopes at the tenant's expense, on the tenant's schedule.

Where the space serves an American-branded product, the option must be conditioned on a Terminal F Reversibility Side Letter to the 2025 U&L amendment before the module locks. That instrument — not a Chief Development Officer memo countersigned by an SVP of Hub Operations — is what turns the register's premium items from preserved capacity the rate base carries into pre-priced options American exercises.

