## The counter-case, honestly presented

The strongest reader in the room will not concede any of this without a fight. Four objections deserve full hearing.

**American signed to 2043. Designing for American's departure is bad faith toward the tenant funding the building.** The 2025 U&L extension gave DFW 18 years of scheduling certainty and made the $4 billion debt stack politically survivable inside the airline relationship. The 82.6% concentration that reads as fragility on a rating-agency methodology sheet also reads as unusually deep alignment through 2043 — and every part of American's public 2026 posture says the hub is being reinforced, not exited. A design report whose spine reads as "encode generic geometry so a future tenant can load it" is, without care, heard as an insurance policy against a partner who is signing checks. The CEO's office will answer for that at the first Fort Worth mayoral event, and the four Fort Worth-appointed board seats — whose bargaining position strengthens as 2043 approaches — will be listening.

**The counterparty for premium is the airline, not the airport.** The industry finance model puts premium on the carrier's side of the line. Delta's JFK T4 Delta One Lounge — roughly 40,000 square feet, 515 seats — is a carrier-funded fit-out on Port Authority base building.[^29] American funded its own Terminal C club and Terminal D Flagship Check-In. If premium at F ever arrives, the standard vehicle is a tenant-improvement program on the airline's balance sheet, timing, and risk. Airport-side preload of "premium-ready bones" inverts the customary allocation, transferring strategy risk from the party with the information to the party without it.

**The reversibility register is a false comfort — options accumulate into a shopping list no future budget funds.** Every preserved option carries a premium: MEP oversizing across gate positions, structural allowance for lounges that never open, curtain-wall breaks for arrival flows the CBP designation never authorizes. The Contrarian's version is not that the register is wrong; it is that the register should have three or four lines, each with a signed AA cost-recovery schedule inside a Side Letter, and refuse everything else. Forty items is a shopping list. Three, priced and countersigned, is discipline.

**The affordability arbitrage is spending down under the airport's feet.** Cost per enplanement moved from $13.59 in FY25 actual to $16.99 in FY26 projected — a roughly 25% single-year jump, primary-document verification pending.[^14] Outstanding debt was projected to grow from $7.2 billion to $12.4 billion by FY29 as of August 2024, before the May 2025 scope expansion added $2.4 billion; the post-expansion figure is unpublished and higher [FC-CHECK: confirm no restated debt trajectory has been published since May 2025].[^15] The 70th Supplemental Bond Ordinance authorizes up to $3.0 billion in new debt through February 28, 2026, and the next new-money authorization requires both Dallas and Fort Worth city-council approval on a calendar the airport does not control. Every dollar of preserved option is a dollar not amortized over gate revenue, and a basis-point ratings slip on the next issuance is a lifetime interest event. The Airline Commercial Strategist's judgment is that signatories change behavior when connecting-hub CPE crosses the low $20s. No public projection places DFW inside that band during the ramp; the trajectory points toward it.

## Why the counter-case is insufficient

The objections survive as constraints on the recommendation, not as defeats of it.

The bad-faith framing misreads what generic geometry insures against. It is not de-hub insurance; it is strategy-shift insurance. Generic geometry protects the tenant, too: the tenant's own next iteration — a further rebank, an international pivot, a merger, a Flagship Arrivals product that does not exist yet — lives inside the same envelope. Reading the register's ownership and cadence to the Fort Worth-appointed seats — with Fort Worth-facing landside, DBE participation, and cargo-side apron geometry (a MARS stand doubles as a widebody freighter position) on the register — keeps it from being heard as a Dallas-side memorandum.

The industry-finance objection is why the register's premium lines must be conditioned on the Side Letter before the module locks. Do not preload airline-funded space on the airport's balance sheet. Do preserve the shell — structural loading, MEP risers, curtain-wall breaks, sterile-corridor and floor-plate dimensions — because the shell is the only piece that must be decided at fabrication release. The fit-out is the airline's line item, on the airline's schedule. DFW should carry the shells and let the fit-outs remain American's.

The shopping-list objection is the discipline the register enforces: a register with forty items fails the Contrarian's test. A register with a small, priced set — the MARS positions, the FIS geometry, a Flagship-convertible shell at a few head-of-pier positions with a countersigned schedule, the 6:15-sized Skylink node, CUPPS hardware, the instrumentation baseline — survives value engineering, board turnover, and the ratings review. Publish it with per-line prices and funding sources. Refuse everything else. The register is not a wish list; it is a stop rule for the wish list.

The affordability objection is why every option needs a number, why the Finance and Audit Committee must set a projected-CPE ceiling the aggregate premium lives inside, and why the premium-adjacent options route through American under Side Letter language that keeps them off the rate base. It is also why under-scoping is usually the more expensive error — the same seven agents' judgment, anchored in three records. IAD's AeroTrain omitted the Concourse D stop, ran 16 years on mobile lounges, and is now folded into a roughly $3.75 billion extension-and-tunnel program inside a $22.5 billion renovation.[^30] Denver's Great Hall paid $184 million to terminate the P3 and $2.1 billion to complete under a new contract.[^31] Heathrow T5 delivered £4.3 billion on time — and opened with a 42,000-bag failure the airport had not commissioned against.[^32] Delta's LGA Terminal C — $4 billion, single-airline, two years early[^33] — is the disciplined counter-example. The affordability constraint does not repeal these lessons. It sharpens them.

Where the counter-case wins outright is on scale: the register short, the premium options conditioned, the affordability envelope governing. The recommendation itself survives the four objections most cleanly — and it is where the tenant's signals across all three readings, the modular arithmetic, and the historical record on this building type each independently point.

