## Implications for the operator

Six decisions fall out of the argument, addressed to specific chairs and sequenced by dependency. All land inside the fabrication-release window that runs through 2030.

**Decision D0 — Confirm the procurement path for template-level changes before any register cycle begins.** *Owner:* Chief Procurement Officer, with the Chief Engineer. *Approval route:* internal disclosure to the Finance and Audit Committee. *First 30-day action:* confirm in writing that the Innovation Next+ JV contract permits owner-directed template changes at repeat-part cost within the current change-order envelope; identify the earliest module release that can still absorb them; confirm the cadence can take them without slipping Terminal C or A. *Cost:* nominal — procurement staff time, from operating budget. *Success measure:* written confirmation, or a written scoping change that acquires the right, before Decision D2 opens. *Failure mode:* D2 and D5 proceed on a procurement assumption the contract does not support, and every template change becomes a re-price event. *Stop condition:* if the contract cannot accommodate repeat-part-cost changes, D5 phases its rollout and D2 restricts the register's cadence accordingly. *What would change the recommendation:* a change in the JV contract, or a competing modular-manufacturer bid.

**Decision D1 — Close the two contract questions the design cannot answer, on a 30-day clock.** *Owner:* General Counsel, with the airline-affairs office. *Approval route:* internal disclosure to the Finance and Audit Committee before the next module's fabrication release. *First 30-day action:* obtain from American a written statement of (a) whether the current U&L amendment obligates a Flagship-tier lounge at Terminal F before 2043 and (b) the MII trigger thresholds for capital additions inside the exclusive-use envelope. The thresholds are confidential and load-bearing on whether the register's premium items are design questions or contract questions. *Cost:* nominal, operating budget. *Success measure:* the Board sees both documents before the next fabrication release. *Failure mode:* the register assumes contract facts not in evidence. *Stop condition:* if American declines, escalate CEO-to-CEO and treat the premium items as conservative design questions bounded by the highest-consent MII assumption. *What would change the recommendation:* the answers themselves.

**Decision D2 — Publish the module-by-module reversibility register as a standing governance artifact, prospectively, against the first module still inside the fabrication window.** *Owner:* Vice President Terminal Development or equivalent — program-manager staffing and a permanent JV liaison, not the CEO's office. *Approval route:* recurring standing item at the Operations, Safety and Security Committee; escalation to the Board on material changes. *First 90-day action, two phases:* Days 0–30, obtain from the JV the per-module release-for-fabrication schedule as contract disclosure — not a request. Days 30–90, name the next module inside the window, publish its pour-locked and still-open decision list with per-item cost order-of-magnitude and funding source (70th Supplemental, a future supplemental requiring both cities' councils, or American cost recovery; PFC unavailable at the $4.50 cap), and hold the first Committee briefing against that module. *Cost:* register staffing $250K–$500K annual loaded — one program manager plus fractional JV-liaison time — from operating budget; option premiums per D3. *Success measure:* every fabrication release preceded by a written register finding; the percentage so preceded reported quarterly. *Failure mode:* the register becomes a decoration on the strategy deck. *Stop condition:* the recommendation-level triad below; on any of the three, the register unwinds on a route the CEO's office publishes. *What would change the recommendation:* MII thresholds (D1); a primary-document CPE projection through FY30; American's written Flagship-at-F statement.

**Decision D3 — Route the premium-adjacent options through American under a Terminal F Reversibility Side Letter, with a Committee-set projected-CPE ceiling.** *Owner:* Chief Development Officer, with General Counsel. *Approval route:* Board memorandum delegating Side Letter execution to the Finance and Audit Committee; on American's side, its internal capital committee — SVP DFW Hub Operations plus the CFO office. *First 90-day action:* draft the Side Letter to the 2025 U&L amendment carrying a schedule of pre-priced options — Flagship-convertible lounge shells at defined head-of-pier positions; a Flagship Arrivals suite provision at up to two international-capable positions on the Skylink spine; CUPPS-capable gate hardware where the exclusive-use envelope permits — with fit-out contingent on American's countersigned exercise, refreshed annually. Set with the Committee a projected-CPE ceiling the aggregate premium may not breach in any FY through FY30. *Cost:* legal and airline-affairs staff time; option premiums per line, published in the register. *Funding:* American cost recovery for premium shells; DFW residual for generic geometry, against the ceiling. *Success measure:* three or fewer premium-conditioned shells, each countersigned, at each fabrication release; aggregate premium inside the ceiling. *Failure mode:* shells accumulate without American's sign-off and become preserved capacity the rate base carries. *Stop condition:* drop after two fabrication cycles without countersign; unwind if the ceiling is breached in the next-cycle forecast. *What would change the recommendation:* American's Flagship-at-F statement and MII thresholds (D1); a change in American's premium build-out schedule.

**Decision D4 — Adopt the 6:15 a.m. degraded-morning stress case as the formal sizing scenario, tabletop it with named federal partners, and re-test annually against the bank-density question.** *Owner:* Chief Operating Officer, with the Chief Engineer and American DFW Hub Operations. *Approval route:* internal program directive; results to the Operations, Safety and Security Committee. *First 90-day action:* rerun Skylink-node vertical circulation, FIS queue depth, checkpoint room geometry, and BHS trunk capacity against the January 2023 winter-event demand curve loaded onto a 13-bank morning; publish results before the next affected module's fabrication release; hold a tabletop with AA DFW Hub Ops, the TSA Federal Security Director, the CBP Port Director, DFW Guest Experience, ARFF, airport police, and Skylink operations. *Cost:* inside COO discretionary and base Terminal F scope; downstream geometry changes carried by D2. *Success measure:* every still-open pour-locked geometry decision documented against the stress case before release; TSA and CBP sign written concurrence on the operating implications. *Failure mode:* the case is invoked once at design intent and forgotten; or the tabletop shows CBP or TSA staffing cannot absorb the sized throughput and the sizing is engineering-only. *Stop condition:* the 13-bank structure is walked back, or the flatter-profile reading is corroborated by two consecutive full-year cycles — the register unwinds the affected geometry. *What would change the recommendation:* two full years of rebank operating data; A321XLR-scale connecting-share erosion; a TSA or CBP written finding.

**Decision D5 — Fix the sensor, cabling, and data-plane schedule at the module template, only after D0 clears the procurement path.** *Owner:* Chief Information Officer, with the Chief Engineer. *Approval route:* Innovation Next+ JV scope-change instrument — not a modular-manufacturer memo; reported to the Operations, Safety and Security Committee. *First 90-day action:* on D0's confirmation, finalize a written spec — dense conduit and pull-boxes on 20-foot centers, fiber and PoE++ backbone at 3–5× current sensor count, camera mounts and data drops at four apron corners and the jetbridge tunnel per gate, fiber diversity out of the terminal, CUPPS-capable gate hardware — and ship it through the factory once, with AA IT in factory witness testing against a live A-CDM, biometric, and CBP stack. *Cost:* modest template premium if D0 clears; materially higher if not. *Funding:* Terminal F base scope if inside the change-order envelope; next supplemental if not. *Success measure:* modular gates delivered against a common instrumentation baseline, factory-tested against the integrated stack. *Failure mode:* per-gate variation eats the repetition dividend and every module is integrated separately. *Stop condition:* factory testing shows the template will not clear repeat-part cost; the rollout phases against modules the JV can absorb without slipping C or A.

**Leading indicators, reported quarterly to the Board.** Percentage of fabrication releases preceded by a written register finding; preserved options exercised versus retired; aggregate option premium as projected CPE contribution against the Committee ceiling; AA connecting share inside the 55%–two-thirds band; rebank peak-density signal from quarter-over-quarter AA DFW departure performance; AA premium build-out on schedule against the published targets; Skylink single-loop transit performance during 22:00–06:00; Side Letter options countersigned versus dropped.

**Board actions requested.** At the next Board meeting: (i) adopt the register as a standing item under the Operations, Safety and Security Committee, with the VP Terminal Development as executive owner; (ii) delegate execution of the Terminal F Reversibility Side Letter to the Finance and Audit Committee; (iii) direct the Committee to set the projected-CPE ceiling before the next new-money supplemental ordinance goes to the Dallas and Fort Worth city councils. At the following meeting: (iv) note the first module's register finding and the tabletop concurrence letter from TSA and CBP; (v) receive General Counsel's summary of the two D1 contract answers and the approved CPE ceiling.

**Stop conditions at the recommendation level.** Three signals unwind the register as a whole. American announces a formal Flagship-tier build-out at Terminal F on a published schedule — a specific fit-out program replaces the register. AA connecting share falls below 55% for two consecutive full-year cycles — the connecting-heavy sizing case is over-committed. U&L renewal negotiations open materially earlier than 2043 — the Side Letter's terms are absorbed into the amendment. The VP Terminal Development convenes the unwind review on any of the three; the CEO's office signs the unwind.

The register does not tell DFW what to build. It tells DFW what remains to be decided, before which date, at what price, from which funding source, against which federal dependency, and inside which contract instrument. It also names the reader's actual customer at Terminal F. Not the Flagship passenger already routed through Terminal D. Not the Admirals Club member headed to Terminal C. The connecting passenger, forty-five to ninety minutes on the ground, walking from a widebody arrival to a domestic departure across a Skylink node at 6:15 a.m. on a bad morning. The building is being made for her. Every option on the register that fails her test should come off. Every option that serves her — quietly, at low marginal cost, at repeat-part discipline through the module template, inside a contract instrument the airport actually holds — is the argument for spending the fabrication-release window well.

The concrete is a decoy. The register is the deadline. The airport-executive layer — the named owner, the real instrument, the confirmed procurement path, the CPE ceiling, the federal-partner concurrence, and the Board's standing route — is the recommendation.

